<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Promptuarium.Serializer</RootNamespace>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- The library implements the experimental PromptuariumType control, so it uses it freely -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);PRMTSER001</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Promptuarium.Serializer</PackageId>
    <Title>Promptuarium.Serializer</Title>
    <Description>A general serializer between .NET objects and Promptuarium trees in the PRMT:CFG1-LE layout: typed holders, byte[] as BLOB, no JSON in between; layering (Populate), records, attributes.</Description>
    <PackageTags>promptuarium serializer serialization configuration binary tree</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>Still experimental (opt-in PRMTSER000). New: CONTAINER (0xF1) in version 2 of the layout (SERIALIZER_FORMAT.md, 6.6): an Element property is stored as the bytes of its whole tree, and read back as an Element (into object too). The value must be one complete stream (not empty, with its terminator, nothing after it); it is decoded within LoadOptions, and its depth counts with MaxDepth when written and read. [PromptuariumType(Container)] on a byte[] stores bytes that are already a tree, checked. A file with a CONTAINER is signed with version 2 (PRMT:GEN2-LE, PRMT:CFG2-LE), whatever the nested tree; 8.4.0 does not read such a file. New: an enum by its number: [PromptuariumType] with an integer type (Int32, UInt8, ...) or Float64 stores its number instead of its name, on a property or on the enum type (for every value of the type); a property can declare another form than its type (Utf8String: the name). Reading takes both forms, as before.</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Promptuarium/Promptuarium.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Promptuarium.Serializer.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
